[{:seon.task/name "web-server-threads-are-joined-at-stop"
  :seon.task/plan "Lane THREADS (2026-10-21): when the web proc binds its port, ring-chez-adapter's :fibers strategy runs its acceptor and its connection sweeper as two futures (two OS threads of Jolt's agent pool, named clojure-agent-pool; target/home/.jolt/gitlibs/https___github.com_jolt-lang_ring-chez-adapter.git/fa0674e/src/ring_chez/adapter.clj:924 start-sweeper!, :1193 the acceptors). The acceptor never blocks in accept(): it polls in accept-poll-ms slices; the sweeper is (Thread/sleep 100) in a loop, a clock (owner, 2026-10-21: no clocks or timers). stop-server (adapter.clj:1363) waits for the acceptor but only flags the sweeper, so after seon.root/stop! the sweeper outlives its owner for up to 100 ms: root's thread count after stop! depends on timing, so bin/check lifecycle runs with SEON_WEB_PORT=0. Correct: the listener is a fiber parked on the listen fd's readiness (jolt.io-poller), idle connections are retired when their own read parks past nothing (a fact, not a sweep), and the web proc's ::flow/stop returns after both are gone. Then seon.root/declared-threads drops its \"clojure-agent-pool\" 2 for a listening web proc, and lifecycle runs with the web on. Replaces: start-sweeper!, the accept poll slice, the SEON_WEB_PORT=0 in bin/check's lifecycle step."
